.TH SH-HOC 1
.SH NAME
hoc \- shell script interface to hoc(2)'s expression language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load hoc

.B hoc reset
.br
.B hoc
.RB [ eqn ]
.I expr...
.br
.B ${hoc
.RB [ eqn ]
.I expr...
.B }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hoc
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR hoc (2)'s
own expression grammar and evaluator \(em nothing more, the same
narrow scope
.IR hoc (2)
itself has
.RI ( compile / compileeqn / eval / symbol / setvar / getvar / reset ).
This module adds no vocabulary of its own beyond
.BR reset :
assignment is already part of hoc's own grammar
.RI ( "x = 3" ),
so there is no separate
.BR set / get
verb either.
.B ${hoc x}
reads a variable,
.B hoc x = 3
(or
.BR "${hoc x = 3}" ,
if the value is wanted too) writes one.
.PP
There is one shared symbol table for the whole shell session, not a
handle table like
.IR sh-pde (1)/ sh-mesh (1)/ sh-fem (1)
have for their own independent problem/mesh handles: those hold
heavyweight, separately steppable simulations a script may want
several of at once, while
.I hoc
here is a desk calculator with one persistent set of variables, the
same single-instance shape
.IR hoc (1)
itself already is. A script wanting a second, independent symbol table
at once can
.B mload
a second namespace (see
.IR sh-mload (1))
and load a second copy of this module into it.
.PP
Every
.I expr
is joined back into one string from its words and handed to
.IR hoc (2)'s
own
.BR compile ()
\(em hoc's own grammar, not
.IR eqn (7)
\(em unless the first word is
.BR eqn ,
in which case the rest goes to
.B compileeqn()
instead
.RI ( eqn (7)
syntax, translated internally; see
.IR hoc (2)
and
.IR eqn2hoc (2)).
The result is then evaluated via
.BR eval ().
The builtin form
.RB ( hoc\  expr ...,
no
.BR "${}" )
evaluates for effect only \(em an assignment's usual reason to appear
as a bare statement, its value discarded; the substitution form
.RB ( "${hoc\  expr ...}" )
yields the value.
.B Hoc reset
starts a fresh symbol table, forgetting every variable \(em the same
.IR hoc (2)
call
.IR hoc (1)
itself makes before a new, unrelated set of expressions.
.PP
An expression containing a shell-special token
.RB ( = ,
for one) must be quoted as a single word, the same as anywhere else in
.IR sh (1);
unquoted,
.B hoc x = 3
is a syntax error before this module ever sees it \(em write
.B "hoc 'x = 3'"
instead.
.PP
A literal expression like
.B x*x
is safe unquoted only because it never changes: an unquoted word
containing
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
or
.B [
is a filename pattern in
.IR sh (1)
(see its own man page), and one that matches nothing simply stands for
itself \(em which is why
.B "${hoc x*x}"
happens to work.  The instant a live value from
.B $var
sits directly next to
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
or
.B [
with no separator, this stops being safe: writing the expression as
.B $h*$d
(no quoting, no
.BR ^ )
can silently misparse \(em observed to raise
.B "sh: null list in concatenation"
or, worse, to silently evaluate against
.IR hoc (2)'s
OWN stale variable of the same name instead of the substituted value,
with no error at all.  Two idioms are safe: build the expression with
.B ^
and single-quoted literal pieces around each live value
.RI ( "'x + h*'^" $d ),
or bind
.IR hoc (2)'s
own persistent variables one at a time with a fully single-quoted,
un-substituted right-hand side and let
.I it
do the arithmetic (see the loop example below) \(em never splice a
raw
.B $var
straight up against
.BR * / ? /[ .
.PP
A complete example:
.EX
	load hoc
	hoc 'x = 3'
	echo xsquared: ${hoc x*x}
	echo eqn form: ${hoc eqn x sup 2 + 1}
	hoc reset

	# binding hoc's own variables across a loop, the safe way -
	# every right-hand side below is a single quoted literal, no
	# live $var ever sits directly against * unquoted
	load std
	hoc 'x = 1.0'
	hoc 'h = 0.25'
	for(i in `{seq 1 4}) {
		d := ${hoc -x}
		hoc 'x = x + h*'^$d
	}
	echo x: ${hoc x}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/hoc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (2),
.IR hoc (1),
.IR eqn2hoc (2),
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-pde (1),
.IR sh-dynsys (1),
.IR sh-mload (1)
.SH BUGS
Two shell processes that each
.B load hoc
get two independent
.IR hoc (2)
instances (per
.IR hoc (2)'s
own two-instances-never-share-a-symbol-table rule) \(em only two
.B load
calls
.I within
one process, via
.IR sh-mload (1),
actually share the ambiguity that rule warns about.
